\cf2\b\fs24 Introduction\par
\cf1\b0\i\f0\fs40 What is the 100% Pure Java Standard?\par
\i0\fs20 The 100% Pure Java standard is the set of guidelines that a programmer can\par
follow to assure a reasonable sense of portability. Customers can be assured\par
that the program relies only on the documented and specified Java platform, so\par
that it will run on any computer hosting a Java Compatible application\par
environment. The end result is that your program delivers on the promise of\par
\ldblquote Write Once, Run Anywhere\f1\'d4\f0 .\rdblquote\par
Programmers can inspect the program for compliance to the standards defined\par
in this guide\i , \i0 using the procedures and test tools provided by Sun.\par
The steps described in this guide are necessary to ensure that your program is\par
portable and that it will meet your customer\rquote s expectations of a product\par
meeting the 100% Pure Java standard.\par
\i\fs32 Purpose\par
\i0\fs20 The purpose of the 100% Pure Java standard is to provide reasonable and\par
economic assurance that a program will run on any Java Compatible platform.\par
Sun addresses these goals by defining the domain where purity is analyzed:\par
\bullet  \b API Constraints \b0 the parts of the Java Class Library that are defined as usable\par
in a pure manner.\par
\bullet  \b Common Behavior \b0 The behavior that your program exhibits on different Java\par
Compatible platforms.\par
\b\fs24 API C\fs19 ONSTRAINTS\par
\b0\fs20 API Constraints describe the rules that Sun has chosen regarding how you call\par
methods defined in the Java Class Library. Only Java Compatible platform\par
API\rquote s can be referenced, and must be used in a portable manner.\par
For more information about API constraints , see "Rules of Purity" in Chapter\par
2, and Chapter 3, "Guidelines for Developing Pure Programs".\par
\b\fs24 C\fs19 OMMON \fs24 B\fs19 EHAVIOR\par
\b0\fs20 An important part of the 100% Pure Java standard is to observe that a program\par
behaves with functional commonality independent of the platform upon which\par
it is run. This means (in principle) that each product feature is demonstrable on\par
any Java Compatible platform. While platforms may exhibit cosmetic user\par
interface and \ldblquote look and feel\rdblquote  differences or have limitations in underlying\par
hardware, operating system, or browser functionality, all product features\par
