\b0\fs16 Is platform portability an important differentiator for my program?\par
A single code base can significantly reduce maintenance costs if you plan to support your\par
program on several hardware platforms. If your product is an applet, servlet, or\par
development library/bean, then you might want to market your code as usable in many/all\par
Java environments.\par
Can my program comply with the purity standard?\par
Use the JavaPureCheck tool to see if there are existing portability problems. It\rquote s free, and\par
takes only a few minutes to run. If problems exist, determine whether they can be fixed or\par
worked around. See \f0\fs18 Chapter 3\b , \b0 "Guidelines for Developing Pure Programs" \f2\fs16 for more\par
information.\par
What if my program can\rquote t comply with the purity standard?\par
Impure programs are not necessarily bad programs. In some cases, it isn\rquote t possible to\par
achieve certain functionality without making direct calls to the operating system or using\par
native code (e.g. using platform-specific devices). Identifying portability problems in your\par
program will, however, make it much easier to port to other hardware platforms. The\par
JavaPureCheck tool provides an excellent means to find potential portability issues in your\par
program.\par
Is complying with the standard worth my effort?\par
A competent engineer should need 3-4 full days (without distractions) to prepare a typical\par
program for for purity compliance. Your time may vary, depending on the nature of your\par
program.\par
Another valuable benefit you might realize is that the process of testing and checking your\par
program with the JavaPureCheck tool might expose bugs or problems in your code.\par
Naturally, it is always best to find these problems \emdash  and fix them \emdash  before your\par
customers find them.\par
\fs20 Revision Date: 10/00 2 - 1\par
\i\f0\fs60 Understanding Portability\par
& Purity \fs72 2\par
\fs40 Standards for Portability\par
\i0\fs20 The ideal of the 100% Pure Java standard is to assure that the end user has a\par
seamless and painless experience running a given Java program on any\par
conforming Java platform. Identical behavior is not the standard; a portable\par
program might behave differently on different platforms, but still exhibit the\par
same feature set.\par
For some programs, portability is not a property of the program, but of the\par
program's input. As an example, consider a program that parses and interprets\par
Java programming language statements. This program will necessarily behave\par
unportably when given an unportable program to interpret. As long as the\par
portability problem is not intrinsic to the program, the program cannot be\par
considered compliant with the 100% Pure Java standard. The software's\par
developer must be the final authority on the program's correct behavior.\par
Similarly, the appearance of the program (if it has a GUI) is the responsibility\par
of the developer. The standard for purity is that the program display\par
essentially the same elements on all platforms, not that those elements have the\par
same appearance.\par
\i\fs32 The Purpose of the Purity Standard\par
\i0\fs20 The purpose of the purity standard is to provide reasonable and economic\par
assurance that the 100% Pure Java program design guidelines have been\par
followed, and that the program will run on any Java Compatible platform. The\par
process is not intended to be immune to subversion, but is intended to be a\par
chance for you to provide evidence of the portability of your product in a\par
uniform and consistent way.\par
\f2 2 - 2 100% Pure Java Cookbook\par
\cf2\b\fs24 Understanding Portability & Purity\par
\cf1\b0\i\f0\fs32 Which Java\i0\fs20\'99 \i\fs32 API?\par
\i0\fs20 Sun Microsystems recommends writing to the 1.1 or later versions of the Java\par
API.\par
The Java technology web site has helpful information about making the\par
transition between v1.0 and v1.1.\par
For information, go to:\par
\f3 http://java.sun.com/products/jdk/1.1/compatible\par
\f0 Unless your circumstances prohibit you from moving away from version 1.0, it\par
is a good idea to do so. You will gain portability benefits along with extra\par
features. In particular, the conformance requirements for 1.1 and later Java\par
platform versions are much more stringent than those that were in place for\par
the 1.0 version; you can thus expect a greater uniformity between platforms\par
implementing 1.1 and later versions than among 1.0 platforms.\par
By writing to the 1.0 interfaces, you achieve backward compatibility with old\par
installations. By writing to the 1.1 or later interfaces, you take advantage of the\par
recent improvements and prepare for the future. The choice depends on your\par
circumstances. Programs written to any version are eligible for certification.\par
The enhancements made to the Java 2 Platform, Standard Edition, v 1.2 API are\par
quite compelling for many developers. Choosing this version of the Java\par
platform (or later) is recommended for all developers who need the state of the\par
art API support found in this version.\par
\i\fs40 Understanding \lquote Purity\rquote\par
\i0\fs20 The Java platform promises streamlined software development and delivery. It\par
saves developers time and it saves their companies the expense of multiple\par
ports and traditional distribution methods. Unlike programming systems that\par
tie developers to a single hardware platform, the Java platform bridges many\par
varieties of hardware and system software with a common language, opening\par
up new markets rather than limiting market opportunities.\par
This portability brings new freedom to developers, who can now deliver\par
solutions for a wide variety of hardware, without the expense and delay of\par
porting and qualification. It also brings new freedom to users, who can choose\par
to change hardware platforms to best meet their needs, while preserving their\par
investment ins software.\par
\f2 Revision Date: 10/00 2 - 3\par
\cf2\b\fs24 Understanding \lquote Purity\rquote\par
\cf1\b0\f0\fs20 Yet the inherent portability of the Java platform alone does not ensure seamless\par
operation for every program. A number of pitfalls can adversely affect the\par
portability of programs, and developers need to know what they are and how\par
to work around them. That is why Sun has developed guidelines for writing\par
100% Pure Java code.\par
A pure program is defined as one that relies only on the documented and\par
specified Java platform. By focusing on this design criterion now, developers\par
can reduce their exposure to portability risks in the future.\par
\i\fs32 Purity is Not Goodness\par
\i0\fs20 Not all good programs are pure; not all pure programs are good.\par
It is entirely possible to use the Java programming language to write a\par
program that depends on platform-specific capabilities, defines native\par
methods, and violates all the rules for purity. Just because a program is not\par
pure does not mean it is poor quality or bad in any way; the program may in\par
fact meet the needs of its users in ways not possible to achieve in a pure\par
manner. An program can be good without being pure.\par
Likewise, the measurements made to ascertain the purity of a program are\par
intentionally blind to the user\rquote s requirements. A program can be completely\par
pure and still be quite unsuited to the user\rquote s requirements \emdash  even be quite\par
buggy. The purity process attempts to find out if the program will behave the\par
same on all Java Compatible platforms, not if it will behave well on all\par
platforms. An program can be pure without being good.\par
Purity is intended to be a measure of only one of the many characteristics\par
required of a program. Although it is not a perfect measure of portability, it is\par
nonetheless a useful measure. Experience has shown that performing purity\par
assessment will detect some common portability problems. In that way,\par
checking the \lquote purity\rquote  of programs does result in better portability of programs.\par
\i\fs32 Portability vs. Purity\par
\i0\fs20 There is a critical distinction\emdash and connection\emdash between portability and purity.\par
Most people think of a portable program as one that produces the same results\par
on any platform. This is actually a very imprecise definition.\par
\f2 2 - 4 100% Pure Java Cookbook\par
\cf2\b\fs24 Understanding Portability & Purity\par
\cf1\b0\f0\fs20 For example, consider this program:\par
\b Figure 2 - 1: Example Code \emdash  Pure Example (class ShowOS)\par
\b0 Most people would say that this is a portable program, even though it\par
produces different results on different platforms. Compare it to this program:\par
\b Figure 2 - 2: Example Code \emdash Impure Example (class BadOS)\par
\b0 The difference between these two programs is not in the use of the Java\par
platform; formally, they are very nearly identical. The difference is in the\par
functionality they implement. The second example contains an OS platform\par
dependency that is implemented into the program in a portable way.\par
\i\fs40 Rules of Purity\par
\i0\fs20 A 100% Pure Java program is one that depends only on the Java platform, as\par
defined in this documentation. It is relatively easy to determine if a program\par
conforms to the design criteria detailed in this manual. If it does, it is defined\par
as \lquote pure.\rquote\par
\f3 class ShowOS \{\par
public static void main (String[] args) \{\par
try \{\par
String osName = System.getProperty("os.name");\par
System.out.println(osName);\par
\} catch (RuntimeException re) \{\par
System.err.println(\ldblquote Problem: " + re);\par
\}\par
\}\par
\}\par
class BadOS\par
public static void main(String[] args) \{\par
try \{\par
String osName = System.getProperty("os.name");\par
if (osName.equals("Solaris")) \{\par
throw new RuntimeException();\par
\}\par
System.out.println(osName);\par
\} catch (RuntimeException re) \{\par
System.err.println("Problem: "+ re);\par
\}\par
\}\par
\}\par
\f2 Revision Date: 10/00 2 - 5\par
\cf2\b\fs24 Rules of Purity\par
\cf1\b0\f0\fs20 Purity is measured at the bottom (platform) edge of the program, rather than at\par
the top (user) edge. Experience has shown us that if a program has been\par
designed according to these standards, in other words, is \lquote pure,\rquote  it is a good\par
predictor of portability; a pure program should not be accidentally unportable.\par
The platform edge is defined by the calls to the Java API (Application Program\par
Interfaces) that the program makes. In this discussion, we will often refer to the\par
\ldblquote Java core API\rdblquote . The core API is the set of documented classes (typically\par
under the \f3 java \f0 and \f3 javax \f0 packages) that Sun designates as public classes.\par
This document sets out the rules and principles for purity, along with hints and\par
tips for the more general goal of portability.\par
\i\fs32 The Rules\par
\i0\fs20 A pure program should:\par
\b 1. Use no native methods.\par
2. Depend only on the Java core Application Programmer Interfaces .\par
3. Rely on no hardwired platform-specific constants.\par
4. Follow any applicable API protocols.\par
\fs28 R\fs22 ULE \fs28 1: U\fs22 SE NO NATIVE METHODS\fs28 .\par
\b0\fs20 The first and foremost rule of purity is to avoid the usage of native methods.\par
Attempting to introduce native code into a program results in the sacrifice of\par
most of the benefits of the Java programming language: security, platform\par
independence, garbage collection, and easy class loading over the network.\par
For users, the security issues of software that includes native methods are\par
substantial. There is no assurance that the code is virus-free; moreover, if a\par
native method has a pointer overrun or attempts to access protected memory, it\par
can crash the Java virtual machine, possibly corrupting and certainly\par
interrupting the user s work.\par
The only exception to this rule is in alternative implementations of standard\par
interfaces. Several of the Java core APIs function as standardized interfaces to\par
specific external functionality; examples are the JDBC\'99 database access\par
interface and the JSA cryptography interface. These are circumscribed\par
interfaces, well-defined and with precise specifications, designed to facilitate\par
free substitution of the user s chosen implementation. Due to this\par
substitutability, a program can include native code implementations of these\par
\f2 2 - 6 100% Pure Java Cookbook\par
\cf2\b\fs24 Understanding Portability & Purity\par
\cf1\b0\f0\fs20 interfaces, as long as it also includes at least one pure implementation. The\par
other implementations may be made available to users as an installation or\par
runtime option.\par
\b\fs28 R\fs22 ULE \fs28 2: D\fs22 EPEND ONLY ON THE \fs28 J\fs22 AVA CORE \fs28 A\fs22 PPLICATION\par
\fs28 P\fs22 ROGRAMMER \fs28 I\fs22 NTERFACES \fs28 .\par
\b0\fs20 The Java core APIs form a standard foundation for components, applets and\par
applications; it is the essential framework for program development. 100%\par
Pure Java programs must depend only on classes and interfaces documented in\par
the Java core API specification.\par
This means, in detail, that a 100% Pure Java program must be a complete\par
program, without dependencies on external libraries or interfaces that are not\par
part of the Java core API specification.\par
The Java core APIs provide the basic language, utility, I/O, network, GUI, and\par
applet services; vendors who have licensed this Java technology from Sun have\par
contracted to include them in any Java platform they deploy.\par
The specifications for all Java APIs are freely accessible on the World Wide Web\par
at http://java.sun.com.\par
\fs24 I\fs19 NCOMPLETE \fs24 P\fs19 ROGRAMS ARE \fs24 I\fs19 MPURE\par
\fs20 To be certified as a program meeting the 100% Pure Java certification standard,\par
must be self-contained; it must include all required classes aside from the core\par
API. An incomplete program is unportable, because it will not run on a Java\par
platform implementation that lacks the special classes required by the\par
program.\par
\fs24 D\fs19 ON\fs24\rquote\fs19 T \fs24 D\fs19 EPEND ON THE \fs24 I\fs19 NTERNALS OF \fs24 A\fs19 NY \fs24 P\fs19 ARTICULAR \fs24 I\fs19 MPLEMENTATION\par
\fs20 Any implementation of the Java core APIs will include classes and packages\par
that are not part of the documented API interface. Portable programs must not\par
depend on these undocumented classes, because they might vary among\par
different Java platform implementations. This is true even if the classes in\par
question are undocumented parts of the reference Java platform\par
implementation from Sun Microsystems. Those interfaces are not part of the\par
Java platform definition, and they are not checked by the Java tests for\par
compatibility, so they might be absent or might behave in subtly and\par
dangerously different ways on different Java platform implementations. They\par
are not documented because they are not intended for client use.\par
\f2 Revision Date: 10/00 2 - 7\par
\cf2\b\fs24 Rules of Purity\par
\cf1\b0\f0\fs20 One subtle way that a program might depend on a class is by defining classes\par
into the packages that are part of the core APIs or a specific implementation.\par
Defining a class within an existing core package is called \ldblquote injecting a class into\par
a core package\rdblquote . This breaks protection boundaries that the core implementors\par
are entitled to count on.\par
Another subtle dependency on implementation details is direct use of the AWT\par
component peer interfaces defined in classes in the \f3 java.awt.peer \f0 package.\par
These interfaces are documented as being for use by AWT implementors; a\par
portable program uses the AWT rather than implementing it.\par
\fs24 U\fs19 SING \fs24 R\fs19 UNTIME\fs24 .\fs19 EXEC\par
\fs20 Using \f3 Runtime.exec \f0 is generally not acceptable as pure. Certain features of\par
the Java programming language definition give the programmer access to\par
hardware-specific code; native method definition (see Rule 3, \ldblquote No Hardwired\par
Platform-Specific Constants\rdblquote ), and some methods in the class\par
\f3 java.lang.Runtime\f0 . This hardware access is very useful for writing\par
programs that interface to legacy systems, but such interface programs are by\par
definition not compliant to the 100% Pure Java standard.\par
The use of the \f3 Runtime.exec \f0 method is only allowed if it\rquote s at the request of\par
the user of the program. For example, a command interpreter that executed\par
programs named in the user\rquote s input could be pure. Another example is the\par
invocation of an external program with a specific function, such as a Web\par
browser, as long as the user has control of which browser is invoked.\par
The exact criteria for use of the \f3 Runtime.exec \f0 method are:\par
\b\fs28\bullet  \b0\fs20 The invocation must be a direct result of a specific user action; the user\par
must know they\rquote re executing a separate program.\par
\b\fs28\bullet  \b0\fs20 The user must be able to choose, by configuration or as part of the\par
invocation action, which program is executed.\par
\b\fs28\bullet  \b0\fs20 A failure of the \f3 Runtime.exec \f0 method, especially one caused by the\par
absence of the requested program, must be handled cleanly.\par
\b\fs28\bullet  \b0\fs20 Use of the \f3 Runtime.exec \f0 method is platform neutral. No platform\par
specific functionality documented by your product can be made\par
exclusively available to users of any particular hardware/OS platform.\par
Examples of the correct use of \f3 Runtime.exec \f0 are:\par
\b\fs28\bullet  \b0\fs20 Invocation of a Java compiler, with the name of the compiler specified as a\par
user-settable Property.\par
\b\fs28\bullet  \b0\fs20 Execution of a command the user typed in (a \ldblquote shell\rdblquote ).\par
\b\fs28\bullet  \b0\fs20 Invocation of a browser, configured as part of the installation of the\par
program, when the user presses a \ldblquote Help\rdblquote  button.\par
\f2 2 - 8 100% Pure Java Cookbook\par
\cf2\b\fs24 Understanding Portability & Purity\par
\cf1\b0\f0\fs20 see Chapter 4, "Explanations of Purity Problems and Variances" for additional\par
information.\par
\fs24 D\fs19 UMMY \fs24 C\fs19 LASSES\par
\fs20 The use of \ldblquote stubs\rdblquote  or \ldblquote dummy classes\rdblquote  to hide references to external classes is\par
prohibited in pure programs. For example, it is considered cheating if you\par
include a class in your program that can be substituted (e.g., via classpath\par
manipulation) with another more functional \emdash  and possibly impure \emdash  class.\par
\b\fs28 R\fs22 ULE \fs28 3: R\fs22 ELY ON NO HARDWIRED PLATFORM\fs28 -\fs22 SPECIFIC\par
CONSTANTS\fs28 .\par
\b0\fs20 This rule prevents programs from using platform-specific constants (such as\par
os.name, os.arch, or os.version) to determine function that your program\par
provides. It is not pure to for a program to provide one set of services for one\par
platform, and a different set of services on another platform.\par
This does not mean that all uses of platform-specific constants are prohibited.\par
For example, testing for and working around platform bugs is encouraged, as\par
long as the solution to the bugs does not provide extra features for a given\par
platform.\par
The \f3 java.io.File \f0 class can be used in an unportable way, by constructing\par
Files using a platform-specific path constant. Similarly, input and output\par
streams can be used unportably, with hard-coded and hardware-specific line\par
termination characters. Fortunately, it is easy to avoid these sources of\par
unportability, because the Java core APIs provide portable alternatives.\par
\b\fs28 R\fs22 ULE \fs28 4: F\fs22 OLLOW \fs28 A\fs22 NY \fs28 A\fs22 PPLICABLE \fs28 API P\fs22 ROTOCOLS\par
\b0\fs20 There are certain methods in the core API that must be called or implemented\par
in a certain pattern. By failing to follow these patterns, it is possible to write a\par
program that is syntactically correct, but which will be highly non-portable.\par
For example:\par
\bullet  AnAWTimplementation is allowed to invalidate a Graphics object after a call to\par
a Component\rquote s update method returns. A Component that retains a reference to\par
the Graphics object may happen to work on one implementation, but is not\par
portable.\par
\bullet  The JavaBeans\'99 component protocol and patterns, which must be followed in\par
order to make a portable bean, are described in the JavaBeans documentation.\par
\bullet  A class that overrides the java.lang.Object equals method must also override the\par
\f3 java.lang.Object.hashCode \f0 method, to preserve the invariant that equal\par
Objects have the same hashCode.\par
\bullet  The 1.1 JDK introduced a new AWT event model; a program should not mix the\par
\f2 Revision Date: 10/00 2 - 9\par
\cf2\b\fs24 Rules of Purity\par
\cf1\b0\f0\fs20 1.0 and 1.1 (or later) AWT event models.\par
\b\i Note: \i0 This rule is not completely detectable, because not all protocolspecified\par
behavior is predictable. This means that the compliance testing\par
will not catch all violations of this rule. Certain applications of this rule,\par
however, do lead to practical measures, and those will be incorporated into\par
the compliance detection.\par
\b0\f2 2 - 10 100% Pure Java Cookbook\par
\cf2\b\fs24 Understanding Portability & Purity\par
\cf1\b0\fs20 Revision Date: 10/00 3 - 1\par
\i\f0\fs60 Guidelines for Developing Pure\par
Programs \fs72 3\par
\fs40 How to Develop a Program\par
\i0\fs20 This section identifies common problems encountered in program\par
programming for portability, and offers solutions or workarounds.\par
This is information about portability, not specifically about purity; some of\par
these portability problems are detected in the purity checking process and\par
some are not. Similarly, some of these pitfalls are specific to given methods of\par
classes, while other pitfalls are stated with principles instead of remedies.\par
\i\fs28 1. Pitfall: Thread Scheduling\par
\i0\fs20 Explanation: Thread scheduling may differ on different platforms. If you rely\par
on priorities or luck to prevent two threads from accessing the same object at\par
the same time, your program is not portable.\par
\f2 3 - 2 100% Pure Java Cookbook\par
\cf2\b\fs24 Guidelines for Developing Pure Programs\par
\cf1\b0\f0\fs20 For example, this program is not portable:\par
\f3 class Counter implements Runnable \{\par
static long val = 0;\par
public void run() \{\par
val += 1;\par
\}\par
public static void main(String[] args)\{\par
try \{\par
Thread t1 = new Thread(new Counter());\par
t1.setPriority(1);\par
Thread t2 = new Thread(new Counter());\par
t2.setPriority(2);\par
t1.start();\par
t2.start();\par
t1.join();\par
t2.join();\par
System.out.println(val);\par
\} catch (Exception e) \{\par
e.printStackTrace();\par
\}\par
\}\par
\}\par
\b\f0 Figure 3 - 1: Example of an Unportable Program\par
\b0 This program might not print \ldblquote 2\rdblquote  on all platforms, even barring errors, because\par
the two threads are not synchronized.\par
Unfortunately, this is a deep problem, and there is no quick check for its\par
presence nor easy fix to prevent it occurring.\par
\b Workaround: \b0 One simple, if drastic, answer is to make all methods\par
synchronized. This may make some synchronization errors show up as obvious\par
deadlocks rather than as silent data corruption. Unfortunately, there are\par
examples of thread contention that will not be detected. For example, the\par
JavaPureCheck Tool will not detect the problem in the example \f3 Counter \f0 class,\par
because the contention in the example is in field access rather than method\par
access.\par
\f2 Revision Date: 10/00 3 - 3\par
\cf2\b\fs24 How to Develop a Program\par
\cf1\f0\fs20 Solution: \b0 Adopt a discipline of multithreaded application programming, as\par
described in several textbooks. One that is specifically written for developers\par
using the Java programming language is \f2 Concurrent Application Programming\par
in Java\f0\'99\f2 , Second Edition, \f0 by Doug Lea, Addison-Wesley 1999,\par
ISBN 0-201-69581-2.\par
\i\fs28 2. Pitfall: Use of Native Code\par
\b\i0\fs20 Explanation: \b0 Calling native functions is inherently not portable. In rare\par
instances however, it may seem necessary to include native methods to deliver\par
access to a system resource that is not supported by the Java technology APIs\par
or to boost performance of a particular program.\par
\b Solutions:\par
\b0\bullet  Define a simple protocol to give that service, then write your 100% Pure Java\par
program as a client of that protocol.\par
\bullet  Rewrite the native method using the Java programming language.\par
It may seem that you could confine the native methods to one class and\par
provide an implementation of that class for every Java platform. However, this\par
\ldblquote solution\rdblquote  in fact only complicates matters, because the number of Java\par
platforms is not fixed, but is ever-increasing; and some Java platforms, such as\par
the JavaStation\'99 workstation, have no ability to execute native program code.\par
There is no 100% Pure Java program workaround for native code. Even though\par
it is possible that a program with native methods can be made portable (by\par
writing a class or method with the Java programming language to serve as a\par
fallback for the native code), such a program cannot be certified as pure\par
because we know no principled way to show that the native-code and Java\par
language versions of such a program actually implement the same functionally.\par
If the two versions do, in fact, implement the same functionality, the program\par
without the native code is an equally capable 100% Pure Java program. That\par
program is the one you can measure purity against, and classify as a 100% Pure\par
Java program.\par
\b\i Note: \i0 The Java Native Method Interface is not a way to make native code\par
platform-independent; it is a way to make it easy to port native code. The\par
native code still must be recompiled for each different hardware, and that\par
recompilation will be difficult or impossible if the target hardware does not\par
provide the library or the capabilities required by the native method.\par
\b0\f2 3 - 4 100% Pure Java Cookbook\par
\cf2\b\fs24 Guidelines for Developing Pure Programs\par
\cf1\b0\i\f0\fs28 3. Pitfall: Use of \i0\f3 exec\par
\b\f0\fs20 Explanation: \b0 The \f3 java.lang.Runtime.exec \f0 method is not generally\par
portable; not all platforms have programs that can be run, and not all\par
platforms have the notion of \ldblquote standard input\rdblquote  or \ldblquote standard output.\rdblquote  A hardcoded\par
program name will not be portable; there is no program that has the\par
same name on all Java platforms.\par
\b Solution\b0 : Don\rquote t use this method except under the restrictions detailed in\par
\ldblquote Using Runtime.exec\rdblquote  in Chapter 2.\par
\i\fs28 4. Pitfall: Failure to Use the Portability Features of Java Core API\par
\b\i0\fs20 Explanation: \b0 It is unportable to hard-code text display sizes, colors, layout\par
management details, etc.\par
\b Solution: \b0 The system properties in general are very useful for portability; use\par
them whenever applicable. The AWT abstracts the details of coping with the\par
platform's window system. Use that abstraction to the fullest. For example:\par
\bullet  Use a \f3 LayoutManager \f0 rather than hard-coding component sizes or positions\par
\bullet  Use the various \f3 getSize \f0 methods\par
\bullet  Use the desktop colors available in \f3 java.awt.SystemColor\f0 .\par
\i\fs28 5. Pitfall: Reflection\par
\b\i0\fs20 Explanation: \b0 The reflection facilities of JDK 1.1 and later versions of the Java\par
platform are an immensely powerful addition to the language; they also make\par
it harder to predict what a program will do. \f3 Method.invoke \f0 can be used to\par
invoke any method, including ones that present portability problems.\par
\b Solution: \b0 Be careful. Any use of \f3 Method.invoke \f0 to start another program\par
must follow the same requirements as \f3 Runtime.exec \f0 detailed in \ldblquote Using\par
Runtime.exe\rdblquote  in Chapter 2.\par
\i\fs28 6. Pitfall: Direct Use of AWT Peer Classes\par
\b\i0\fs20 Explanation: \b0 Portable programs, that use rather than implement the AWT\par
interfaces, should not use the AWT peer classes directly. The protocol for\par
interaction with the peer classes is platform specific.\par
\b Solution: \b0 Stay on the client side of the AWT.\par
\f2 Revision Date: 10/00 3 - 5\par
\cf2\b\fs24 How to Develop a Program\par
\cf1\b0\i\f0\fs28 7. Pitfall: Misuse of System.exit\par
\b\i0\fs20 Explanation: \b0 The \f3 System.exit \f0 method forces termination of all threads in\par
the Java virtual machine. This is drastic. It might, for example, destroy all\par
windows created by the interpreter without giving the user a chance to record\par
or even read their contents.\par
\b Solution: \b0 Programs should usually terminate by stopping all non-daemon\par
threads; in the simplest case of a command-line program, this is as easy as\par
returning from the main method. \f3 System.exit \f0 should be reserved for a\par
catastrophic error exit, or for cases when a program is intended for use as a\par
utility in a command script that may depend on the program\rquote s exit code.\par
\i\fs28 8. Pitfall: Use of Hard-Coded File Paths\par
\b\i0\fs20 Explanation: \b0 Hard-coded filenames may present portability problems. Hardcoded\par
paths (with directory names joined to filenames) certainly do.\par
\b Solution: \b0 The most portable way to construct a \f3 File \f0 for a file in a directory is\par
to use the \f3 File(File,String) \f0 constructor to build up the path. Other\par
portable solutions are to use the system properties to get the local file separator\par
and starting directory, or to use a file dialog to ask the user for a filename.\par
\b\i Note: \i0 The concept of an \ldblquote absolute path\rdblquote  is somewhat system dependent; for\par
example, UNIX\b0\f1\fs16\'e2 \b\f0\fs20 absolute paths all start with \ldblquote /\rdblquote , while Windows absolute\par
paths may start with any letter. For this reason, the use of an absolute path\par
that is not derived from user input or from a system property is unportable.\par
\b0 Here is the code for a utility class that might make it more convenient to\par
construct portable pathnames:\par
\f3 package util;\par
import java.io.File;\par
import java.util.StringTokenizer;\par
/** A utility class to make it easier to use\par
* java.io.File in a portable way.\par
*/\par
public class FileUtil \{\par
/** Create a new pathname by gluing together\par
* a series of names.\par
* If initial base is null, works from\par
* current directory.\par
*/\par
public static File fromDir(File bse,\par
