\i\f0\fs28 9. Pitfall: JDBC\i0\'99\i Driver Loading\par
\b\i0\fs20 Explanation: \b0 The JDBC interface, defined by the \f3 java.sql \f0 package, provides\par
for flexibility in loading the actual JDBC driver code. This flexibility allows for\par
substitution of different JDBC drivers without changes to the programs code.\par
This flexibility is provided by the \f3 DriverManager \f0 class, which selects among\par
the available JDBC drivers during connection establishment. Drivers can be\par
made available to the DriverManager in two ways:\par
\bullet  They can be named in the \f3 jdbc.drivers \f0 system property\par
\bullet  They can be explicitly loaded by use of the \f3 java.lang.Class.forName\par
\f0 method.\par
This becomes a portability issue because JDBC drivers, particularly those that\par
include native code, might be less portable than the programs that uses them.\par
If you code a specific JDBC driver name into your program, your program is\par
only as portable as that driver. To be pure, your program must be certified with\par
a portable driver.\par
\b Solution: \b0 In order to maximize the portability of your JDBC program, we\par
recommend that you make the exact JDBC driver name configurable. You can\par
do this either by relying on the \f3 jdbc.drivers \f0 system property, or by using a\par
property file (or some other configuration mechanism) to provide the name of\par
the JDBC driver class that will be loaded with the \f3 Class.forName \f0 method.\par
It is also possible to load a selection of drivers, relying on the \f3 DriverManager\par
\f0 selection mechanism to find an appropriate driver when the database\par
connection is made.\par
\f2 3 - 8 100% Pure Java Cookbook\par
\cf2\b\fs24 Guidelines for Developing Pure Programs\par
\cf1\b0\f0\fs20 If you chose to use this approach, consider the following guidelines:\par
\bullet  Drivers are tried in the order they were registered, so earlier drivers will have\par
priority over later ones, with drivers listed in \f3 jdbc.drivers \f0 having highest\par
priority.\par
\bullet  Adriver that includes native code will fail to load on any platform other than the\par
one for which it was written; therefore, your program must cope gracefully with\par
the ensuing \f3 ClassNotFoundException\f0 ,\par
\bullet  Adriver with native code must not register itself with the \f3 DriverManager \f0 until\par
it knows that it has successfully loaded.\par
\bullet  A driver with native code is not governed by the security sandbox, and presents\par
a potential security hazard.\par
In addition, relying on \f3 Class.forName \f0 to run the static section of the driver\par
(which typically creates a driver instance and registers it) causes portability\par
problems. The workaround is to explicitly create and register an instance of the\par
driver class, like this:\par
\f3 DriverManager.register(\par
Class.forName("MyDriver").newInstance());\par
\f0 Since the static section may run, and may register the class, this can result in\par
duplicate instances and duplicate registrations; however, according to the\par
JDBC team this should not create a problem.\par
\i\fs28 10. Pitfall: Line Termination\par
\b\i0\fs20 Explanation: \b0 Different platforms have different conventions for line\par
termination in a text file. This is a common instance of the general problem of\par
representing text.\par
Different machines have different internal representations of text. The Java\par
platform uses Unicode internally, which is an international standards-based\par
solution to the problem; but we still need to get text to and from files.\par
The Java 1.1 (and later) API has the \f3 java.io.Reader \f0 and \f3 java.io.Writer\par
\f0 classes to handle that kind of character-set conversion. However, the problem\par
can arise even when reading and writing plain ASCII files, because the ASCII\par
standard isn't specific about the line termination character. Some machines use\par
"\f3\\n\f0 ", some use "\f3\\r\f0 ", some use the sequence "\f3\\r\\n\f0 ". The portable way to deal\par
with this, so you'll write files that the user can read, is to use the "\f3 println\f0 "\par
methods to write a line of text, or to put the line marker at the end of a line.\par
You may also use the \ldblquote\f3 line.separator\f0\rdblquote  system property (using the System\par
properties may be problematic for applets that can not access the System for\par
security reasons).\par
\b Solution: \b0 For input, rather than writing the code yourself to look for the\par
various line-termination sequences, it's best and easiest to use the \f3 readLine\par
\f0 method from the \f3 java.io.BufferedReader \f0 class to fetch a complete line of\par
\f2 Revision Date: 10/00 3 - 9\par
\cf2\b\fs24 How to Develop a Program\par
\cf1\b0\f0\fs20 text. The other \f3 readLine \f0 methods are also useful, but the one in\par
\f3 BufferedReader \f0 gives you code set translation as well. Likewise, for output,\par
use \f3 writeLine or println \f0 function to output lines of text.\par
\i\fs28 11. Pitfall: Unportable Command-Line Programs\par
\b\i0\fs20 Explanation: \b0 Command-line programs that use \f3 System.in, System.out\f0 ,\par
or \f3 System.err \f0 might be less than perfectly portable, because not all Java\par
platforms have the concept of standard input or output streams.\par
\b Solution: \b0 Consider using a GUI, at least as an alternative \emdash  some platforms\par
don't have a command line.\par
\i\fs28 12. Pitfall: Command Line Processing\par
\b\i0\fs20 Explanation: \b0 The Java platform leaves command line processing up to the\par
programmer. However, the syntax and conventions are quite different on the\par
different platforms.\par
\b Solution: \b0 The most portable answer is not to use the command line, but that's\par
no good for batch programs that need to be driven from a script. Use the\par
widely understood POSIX convention (options indicated with a dash) when\par
processing command line options. Provide, at least as an alternative, a GUI, or\par
read options from properties files (and document the properties).\par
\i\fs28 13. Pitfall: Internationalization\par
\b\i0\fs20 Explanation: \b0 Computing is a worldwide phenomenon. Particularly if your\par
program is an applet, it is highly likely to be run in an environment with a\par
different native language than yours.\par
\b Advice: \b0 Use the internationalization and localization features of JDK\fs28\'99\fs20 1.1\par
software and later Java platform versions. Complete documentation is\par
included in the JDK software, and is also available at:\par
\f3 http://java.sun.com/products/jdk/1.1/docs/guide/intl\par
\f0 and\par
\f3 http://java.sun.com/products/jdk/1.2/docs/guide/intl\par
\i\f0\fs28 14. Pitfall: Unicode Rendering\par
\b\i0\fs20 Explanation: \b0 Not all platforms can render all Unicode characters.\par
\f2 3 - 10 100% Pure Java Cookbook\par
\cf2\b\fs24 Guidelines for Developing Pure Programs\par
\cf1\f0\fs20 Workaround: \b0 For the default text of messages, buttons, labels, and menus, use\par
only ASCII. Of course, it is all right to use non-ASCII in localization resources\par
and in text obtained from the user.\par
\i\fs28 15. Pitfall: File I/O\par
\b\i0\fs20 Explanation: \b0 The JDK 1.0 input and output classes are not portable to\par
hardware architectures with non-ASCII native file formats.\par
\b Solution: \b0 Use the reader & writer classes found in the JDK 1.1 software and\par
later versions of the Java platform.\par
\i\fs28 16. Pitfall: GUI Element Size\par
\b\i0\fs20 Explanation: \b0 The exact size of the AWT elements will differ from platform to\par
platform, as will the size of the screen and the default and maximum size of a\par
window. Any hard-coded positions or sizes will run afoul of these variations.\par
\b Solution: \b0 Use a layout manager.\par
\i\fs28 17. Pitfall: GUI Fonts\par
\b\i0\fs20 Explanation: \b0 The size and availability of fonts varies from display to display\par
(even on the same hardware platform, depending on installation).\par
\b Solution: \b0 Don\rquote t hard-code text sizes; let text elements assume their natural size\par
in a layout, and use the FontMetrics methods to find the actual displayed size\par
of a string on a Canvas. When setting a nondefault font, be sure to implement\par
a fallback in the \ldblquote catch\rdblquote  block. When creating a font menu, get the font names\par
from the \f3 java.awt.Toolkit.getFontList \f0 method rather than using a\par
hardwired font list.\par
When updating a program from 1.0 to 1.1 or later versions of the Java platform,\par
be sure to update the font names as described in the documentation for the\par
\f3 java.awt.Toolkit.getFontList \f0 method:\par
\bullet  \f3 Times Roman \f0 becomes \f3 Serif\par
\f0\bullet  \f3 Helvetica \f0 becomes \f3 Sans-serif\par
\f0\bullet  \f3 Courier \f0 becomes \f3 Monospaced\par
\i\f0\fs28 18. Pitfall: GUI Appearance\par
\b\i0\fs20 Explanation: \b0 The size of the screen and the number of available colors may\par
change from platform to platform, or even user-to-user or day-to-day. This can\par
make a display illegible (for example, black text on a dark blue background), or\par
can hide buttons if they display off the screen.\par
\f2 Revision Date: 10/00 3 - 11\par
\cf2\b\fs24 How to Develop a Program\par
\cf1\f0\fs20 Solution: \b0 It may be necessary to adjust font size or default window size\par
according to screen resolution, which may be obtained by:\par
\f3 Toolkit tk = java.awt.Toolkit.getDefaultToolkit();\par
int dpi = tk.getScreenResolution();\par
\f0 Applets are probably safest to use the default font, as it may have been\par
customized by the user according to their personal preferences.\par
For an application, you may wish to give the user an option or control to select\par
among several appearances, so they can choose one that suits their display and\par
mood.\par
If writing to the JDK 1.1 software or later interface, you can make your colors\par
harmonize with the user\rquote s desktop by using the colors from the\par
\f3 java.awt.SystemColor \f0 class.\par
If you choose to use your own color scheme instead, beware that displays vary\par
greatly in the color displayed for a particular RGB triple. Your program\rquote s\par
appearance will be more portable if you use named color fields from\par
\f3 java.awt.Color \f0 rather than numeric colors.\par
\i\fs28 19. Pitfall: The Paint Protocol\par
\b\i0\fs20 Explanation: \b0 The AWT \f3 Component.paint \f0 and \f3 Component.update \f0 methods\par
take a Graphics object as a parameter. This object should not be retained,\par
because it might be a transient object valid only during the paint; an AWT\par
implementation is free to destroy that Graphics object after the paint method\par
returns, which makes it pointless (and dangerous) to retain the object.\par
\b Solution: \b0 Do not retain the paint Graphics object. In general, it is not safe to\par
paint outside a call to \f3 update() \f0 (or \f3 paint()\f0 , which is called by the default\par
\f3 Component.update()\f0 ). If you do need a long-lived Graphics object, you\par
should create a new one from the argument value, but be warned that this\par
might fail on some platforms:\par
\f3 Graphics myGraphics = null; // retained\par
void paint(Graphics g) \{\par
if (myGraphics == null) \{\par
myGraphics = g.create();\par
\}\par
// painting code goes here\par
\}\par
\f2 3 - 12 100% Pure Java Cookbook\par
\cf2\b\fs24 Guidelines for Developing Pure Programs\par
\cf1\b0\i\f0\fs28 20. Pitfall: Mixed Event Models\par
\b\i0\fs20 Explanation: \b0 The JDK 1.1 software (and later) AWT uses a different event\par
model from the previous AWT. Programs written to the 1.0.2 event model will\par
work on a 1.1 or later platform version, but mixing the two event models in\par
one program will almost certainly not work.\par
\b Solution: \b0 Stick to one event model per program. Don\rquote t try to convert a\par
program gradually from the old event model to the new; do it all at once.\par
\i\fs28 21. Pitfall: Use of Deprecated Methods\par
\b\i0\fs20 Explanation: \b0 Certain methods from the Java core APIs have been marked as\par
deprecated. While these methods work in the current release, they are slated\par
for removal at some point.\par
\b Advice: \b0 When you work on code (class, method, or field) that uses deprecated\par
methods, it is probably a good idea to update the code for the replacement as\par
suggested in the API documentation. This will also give you a head start on\par
future work.\par
\i\fs28 22. Pitfall: The \i0\f3\fs29 Object.hashCode\i\f0\fs28 and \i0\f3\fs29 Object.equals\par
\i\f0\fs28 Methods\par
\b\i0\fs20 Explanation: \b0 The \f3 Object.hashCode \f0 method returns a number which is\par
effectively random. The number is also implementation- and instancedependent.\par
This has several consequences:\par
\bullet  The order of iteration for elements in a \f3 Hashtable \f0 will be unpredictable, and\par
will generally differ from one program invocation to the next.\par
\bullet  Aclass that overrides \f3 Object.equals \f0 must also override \f3 Object.hashCode\f0 .\par
It is safe, if inefficient, if the \f3 hashCode \f0 method returns the same value for\par
unequal objects. It is unsafe if the \f3 hashCode \f0 method returns different values for\par
two objects that are, in fact, equal.\par
\b Solution: \b0 If a repeatable (although unpredictable) order of enumeration over\par
the elements in a \f3 Hashtable \f0 is important to your program, then any object\par
used as a key in the \f3 Hashtable \f0 should have a class-specific \f3 hashCode\par
\f0 method which returns a value computed from data fields of the object.\par
If any of your classes defines an equals method, it must also define a\par
\f3 hashCode \f0 method, such that for any two objects \f3 a,b \f0 of that class,\par
\f3 a.equals(b) \f0 implies that \f3 a.hashCode() == b.hashCode()\f0 . Note that\par
hashCode should not depend on any mutable property. If an object\rquote s\par
\f3 hashCode \f0 value changes, that object is very likely to become unfindable.\par
\f2 Revision Date: 10/00 3 - 13\par
\cf2\b\fs24 How to Develop a Program\par
\cf1\b0\i\f0\fs28 23. Pitfall: Installation Issues\par
\b\i0\fs20 Explanation: \b0 There are a series of problems that can arise when installing on\par
various platforms, due to limitations or restrictions on filenames. This might\par
interfere with the convention that the Java virtual machine uses to locate\par
required class files, which depends on a simple mapping from class name to\par
filename.\par
The problems might occur when installing, or when attempting to run the\par
installed program.\par
The problematic restrictions are:\par
\bullet  \b File name length: \b0 This is particularly a problem with inner classes, which are\par
represented as class files with concatenated names.\par
\bullet  \b Case distinctions: \b0 Some platforms ignore case when comparing filenames. If you\par
have two classes\emdash or a class and a package\emdash whose names differ only by case,\par
your program will not be portable to those platforms.\par
\bullet  \b Imperfect Unicode Support: \b0 Class names may contain Unicode characters that\par
are not usable as filenames on all platforms.\par
\bullet  \b Special Filenames: \b0 Some platforms assign special meaning to certain filenames,\par
such as \ldblquote\f3 LPT\f0\rdblquote  or \ldblquote\f3 con\f0\rdblquote . These filenames cannot be used as part of a package\par
name for classes that are to be installed as files on those systems.\par
\b Workarounds: \b0 Package your classes into a \f3 .jar \f0 archive, which is part of the\par
JDK, starting with version 1.1. This will work around the problems of long\par
class names and of case distinctions. It might not work around the problem of\par
non-ASCII class names.\par
If you are writing for the JDK 1.0 platform, a \f3 .zip \f0 file is a possible alternative.\par
\b Solution: \b0 Either rename your packages and classes, or use a \f3 .jar \f0 or \f3 .zip \f0 file.\par
\i\fs28 24. Pitfall: Hostname Format\par
\b\i0\fs20 Explanation: \b0 The format of the string returned by the\par
\f3 java.net.InetAddress.getHostName \f0 method depends on the hardware\par
platform. In some cases, it will be a fully-qualified domain name; in others, it\par
will only be the host part of that name.\par
\b Workaround: \b0 In most cases, this problem, which is a case of underspecification,\par
will pose no practical problem, because the unqualified name and\par
the fully-qualified name can be used for identification and connection within a\par
domain. In cases where the name must be exported to distant hosts, it may be\par
best to give the IP number in addition to the host name. The IP number is\par
available from the \f3 getAddress \f0 method.\par
\f2 3 - 14 100% Pure Java Cookbook\par
\cf2\b\fs24 Guidelines for Developing Pure Programs\par
\cf1\b0\i\f0\fs28 25. Pitfall: Pluggable Look and Feel\par
\b\i0\fs20 Explanation: \b0 The Pluggable Look and Feel (PLAF) architecture built into the\par
Swing classes of the Java 2 Standard Edition and the Java Foundation Classes\par
standard extension for JDK 1.1 software allows windows, dialogs and other\par
GUI components to take on a distinctive visual identity called a LookAndFeel.\par
Not all PLAFs are available on every platform and some may only be\par
supported on the operating system the PLAF emulates. Portable programs\par
should ensure that a PLAF is both supported and available to the underlying\par
Java platform before it is set.\par
\b\i Note: \i0 The Java platform Look and Feel is the default LookAndFeel and is\par
always available. It will be used if the requested PLAF is not available or\par
unsupported.\par
Advice: \b0 For method:\par
\f3 javax.swing.UIManager.setLookAndFeel(javax.swing.LookAnd\par
Feel)\par
\f0 Make sure that the specified PLAF class is supported on the active Java\par
platform by using \f3 LookAndFeel.isNativeLookAndFeel() \f0 or use the\par
current PLAF class from method \f3 UIManager.getLookAndFeel()\f0 .\par
For method:\par
\f3 javax.swing.UIManager.setLookAndFeel(java.lang.String)\par
\f0 Use \f3 UIManager.getSystemLookAndFeelClassName() \f0 or\par
\f3 UIManager.getCrossPlatformLookAndFeelClassName() \f0 to ensure that\par
the PLAF class is supported.\par
Alternatively, you can call \f3 UIManager.getInstalledLookAndFeels()\par
\f0 This returns a \f3 LookAndFeelInfo \f0 object. This object can be queried to\par
determine which PLAFs are available to the currently active Java platform.\par
You must be careful when designing a user interface based upon a particular\par
PLAF since your user interface might be compromised if your program is run\par
on a platform where that PLAF is unavailable. To be safe, check your user\par
interface with the Java platform Look and Feel unless you are certain the\par
targeted PLAF will always be available.\par
Obviously, the most portable PLAF is the default Java Platform Look and Feel.\par
\f2 Revision Date: 10/00 3 - 15\par
\cf2\b\fs24 How to Develop a Program\par
\cf1\b0\i\f0\fs28 26. Pitfall: Mixing Classes Compiled on Different Versions of the Java\par
Platform\par
\b\i0\fs20 Explanation: \b0 Classes are compiled against a particular API set defined by the\par
Java platform version. A program compiled on one major version (i.e., 1.0, 1.1,\par
1.2, etc.) of the Java platform might not run on earlier versions because the\par
program might target APIs that do not exist in earlier versions.\par
Mixing classes compiled against different major versions of the Java platform is\par
generally not recommended because, in some rare situations, bug fixes or\par
subtle incompatibilities between the versions may cause portability problems.\par
\b\i Note: \i0 When the JavaPureCheck tool is used to check a program using the 1.2\par
API system model, it will generate a warning when the program references a\par
method from JDK 1.1 software that has been removed in the 1.2 API. The 1.2\par
virtual machine will properly resolve this reference if the superclass\par
contains a method with the same signature. However, the JavaPureCheck\par
tool does not resolve these references to the superclass, and it generates a\par
warning to flag the use of a class compiled on an earlier Java platform\par
version.\par
\b0 Sometimes you might be forced to include a library or \f3 .jar \f0 of class files\par
compiled against JDK1.1 software with a program that is compiled against 1.2.\par
In this case, the warnings are unavoidable.\par
\b Advice: \b0 If possible, compile all class files against the same Java platform\par
version on which you plan to run your program. For situations where you\par
must make use of classes that were compiled against earlier versions of the\par
Java platform, check the relevant compatibility page for the Java development\par
kit:\par
\f3 http://java.sun.com/products/jdk/1.1/compatibility.html\par
http://java.sun.com/products/jdk/1.2/compatibility.html\par
\f0 If you have difficulty with a 3rd party class library, contact the vendor and ask\par
to have a library compiled for your target Java platform version.\par
\b\i Note: \i0 Compiling your classes with the \f4 -target 1.1 \f0 compiler flag on\par
version 1.2 only affects the class file format; it does not change the version of\par
the JDK software APIs that the compiler compiles against.\par
\b0\f2 3 - 16 100% Pure Java Cookbook\par
\cf2\b\fs24 Guidelines for Developing Pure Programs\par
\cf1\b0\i\f0\fs40 Portability Hints\par
\i0\fs20 This section presents general hints for writing portable code.\par
\i\fs28 1. Hint: Writing Portable Applets\par
\i0\fs20 Writing portable applets is somewhat harder than writing portable programs.\par
An applet is, formally, a class that extends \f3 java.applet.Applet\f0 . In\par
actuality, an applet's portability situation includes the Web page the applet\par
loads from, the other classes the applet uses, the HTML that loads the applet,\par
and the security manager and AppletContext of the user's browser.\par
One subtle, possible problem in the HTML \f3 <applet> \f0 tag is that the contents\par
of the applet markup must follow these rules:\par
\bullet  \f3 <param> \f0 elements come before the alternate contents\par
\bullet  alternate contents are text elements, such as the contents of a paragraph (the \f3 <p>\par
\f0 tag). This means that you cannot use \f3 <p> \f0 tags in the alternate contents!\par
Applets will almost certainly have to run under control of a security manager.\par
However, there is no standard profile for security managers. The user can\par
instruct their security manager to deny any combination of access. The best\par
tactic is to make sure that your applet handles any security exception\par
gracefully.\par
Similarly, the Java technology API specifications do not specify required\par
content types or protocols. The MIME types \f3 image/jpeg \f0 and \f3 image/gif\par
\f0 are probably safe, as are the \f3 http\f0 :, \f3 file\f0 :, and \f3 ftp\f0 : protocols. Again, handle\par
any errors gracefully.\par
In the Java 1.0 technology API, the specification of the \f3 AppletContext\f0 /\par
\f3 Applet \f0 protocol was not very precise. As a consequence, different browsers\par
call the applet\rquote s enter & leave methods at different times. The protocol\par
specification is much more precise in the Java 1.1 software and later API, so\par
this problem will disappear in time. Meanwhile, beware that an applet that\par
behaves politely on one browser may hog resources on another, while a\par
different applet may function normally on one browser but stall on another.\par
\i\fs28 2. Hint: Security Exceptions\par
\i0\fs20 If your program will run under control of a \f3 SecurityManager \f0\emdash  for\par
example, if it's an applet \emdash  it might encounter a \f3 SecurityException\f0 . This is\par
an unchecked exception, so it may be thrown without being declared.\par
\f2 Revision Date: 10/00 3 - 17\par
\cf2\b\fs24 Portability Hints\par
\cf1\b0\f0\fs20 Depending on the particular \f3 SecurityManager\f0 , the user can restrict any\par
access to protected resources attempted by the program. Therefore, it will\par
greatly enhance the portability of your program if you catch any\par
\f3 SecurityException \f0 and report the situation to the user in a polite manner.\par
\i\fs28 3. Hint: Coping with Bugs\par
\i0\fs20 There are bugs in some Java platform implementations. To be most portable, it\par
is courteous to work around those bugs. It is helpful to consult the known bug\par
lists available from Sun and from some browser makers. Bugs are harder to\par
cope with than features, because they are less well defined and less well\par
designed.\par
\b Workarounds: \b0 In some cases, a program will have to include different code for\par
different platforms. Workarounds are expensive, both to code and to test. They\par
are, unfortunately, inescapable in some circumstances.\par
In general, you can activate a workaround either proactively by branching on\par
system properties, such as \f3 java.class.version \f0 and \f3 os.name\f0 , or reactively\par
by putting the workaround in an exception handler. The choice depends on the\par
character of the workaround. If the structure of the program or of long-lived\par
data structures is affected, it\rquote s probably best to be proactive. If the workaround\par
has relatively local effect, a reactive fallback may be easiest to code and\par
understand. When possible, the reactive fallback will probably perform better\par
when the fallback is not taken, because the cost of establishing an exception\par
handler is quite low.\par
A preemptive fallback is required if the normal code path would cause\par
irreversible changes before the problem is detected.\par
Here\rquote s an example of a preemptive workaround:\par
\f3 class Preemptive \{\par
private boolean hasFooBug() \{\par
String java_name =\par
System.getProperty(\ldblquote java.maker\rdblquote );\par
return java_name.equals(\ldblquote KnownBad\rdblquote );\par
\}\par
void something() \{\par
if (hasFooBug()) \{\par
workaround();\par
\} else \{\par
// normal code path\par
\f2 3 - 18 100% Pure Java Cookbook\par
\cf2\b\fs24 Guidelines for Developing Pure Programs\par
\cf1\b0\f3\fs20\}\par
\}\par
\}\par
\b\f0 Figure 3 - 1: class Preemptive\par
\b0 And here is an example of a reactive workaround:\par
\f3 class Reactive \{\par
void something() \{\par
try \{\par
// normal code path\par
\} catch (Exception e) \{\par
workaround();\par
\}\par
\}\par
\}\par
\b\f0 Figure 3 - 2: class \f4 Reactive\par
\b0\f2 Revision Date: 10/00 4 - 1\par
\i\f0\fs60 Explanations of Purity Problems\par
and Variances \fs72 4\par
\fs40 Explanations of Purity Problems\par
\i0\fs20 For a detailed HTML-generated list of the purity problems, use the following\par
command line to run the JavaPureCheck tool to generate a report:\par
\f3 java JavaPureCheck -d <model> -h\par
\f0 where \f3 <model> \f0 is: \f3 jdk102\f0 , \f3 jdk11 \f0 or \f3 jdk12\par
\f0 In this usage, the JavaPureCheck tool creates an html description file of the\par
system model JavaPureCheck loads for a given JDK version..\par
\i\fs32 Report Information Categories\par
\i0\fs20 The report categorizes reports into three categories:\par
\bullet  \b Pure: \b0 No portability problems. The class file is a pure Java class.\par
\bullet  \b Warning: \b0 Possible portability problem. You must provide an explanation for all\par
warnings.\par
\bullet  \b Error: \b0 An unequivocal portability problem. An error must be granted a variance\par
by Sun. The only acceptable explanation for such an error is a reference to a\par
variance number granted by Sun.\par
\f2 4 - 2 100% Pure Java Cookbook\par
\cf2\b\fs24 Explanations of Purity Problems and Variances\par
\cf1\b0\i\f0\fs32 Explanations\par
\i0\fs20 You should provide explanations for all warnings and errors identified on the\par
JavaPureCheck tool report. An explanation must describe why the reported\par
problem does not, in fact, impede portability. Below is a list of typical problems\par
reported by the JavaPureCheck tool, the reason they were flagged, and an\par
indication of how to handle them:\par
\b\i Note: \i0 See the documentation included with the JavaPureCheck tool and the\par
information below for complete coverage of all warnings and errors.\par
\b0\i\fs32 Warnings\par
\fs28 Warning: Possible Hardcoded Path\par
\i0\fs20 The JavaPureCheck tool checks the string constants in your application,\par
looking for any that resemble hardcoded file pathnames. The JavaPureCheck\par
tool attempts to resolve any suspicious strings by determining how they are\par
used. If a suspicious string (one that looks like a hardcoded path) is used in\par
an I/O class constructor, this is reported as an error. If the JavaPureCheck\par
tool cannot determine how a suspicious string is used, it reports a warning.\par
If the string identified in the JavaPureCheck tool report is not a hardcoded\par
path, you should supply an explanation that describes how the string is\par
used. Strings used as URLs, for example, are acceptable.\par
\i\fs28 Warning: Mixing the 1.0 and 1.1 Event Models\par
\i0\fs20 This is likely to be a application programming error that will impair\par
portability.\par
Possible explanations for this warning would have to show reasonable need\par
for mixing event models.\par
\i\fs28 Warning: Dynamically Loaded Classes:\par
\i0\fs20\bullet  Use of \f3 java.lang.Class.forName\par
\f0\bullet  Use of \f3 java.lang.ClassLoader.defineClass\par
\f0\bullet  Use of \f3 java.lang.ClassLoader.resolveClass\par
\f0\bullet  Use of \f3 java.lang.ClassLoader.findSystemClass\par
\f0 Classes that are dynamically loaded might contain portability bugs.\par
\f2 Revision Date: 10/00 4 - 3\par
\cf2\b\fs24 Explanations of Purity Problems\par
\cf1\b0\f0\fs20 To explain an acceptable use of these methods, you should list all the classes\par
that could be dynamically loaded such that you can determine that those\par
classes are part of the systembeing checked for compliance. All classes\par
should be checked for purity.\par
If your application does dynamic loading of arbitrary classes, you should\par
provide a description of how the names of those classes are determined.\par
\i\fs28 Warning: Object is Compiled for a Previous (Java Development Kit)\par
release\par
\i0\fs20 This warning appears when your class files are compiled on one version of\par
the Java platform, and have dependencies on methods that no longer exist\par
when tested on a later system model by the JavaPureCheck tool.\par
Even though the Java virtual machine will handle these situations by\par
deferring execution of a missing method to its superclass, mixing classes\par
compiled against different versions of the Java platform is discouraged\par
because, in rare situations, bug fixes or subtle incompatibilities between the\par
versions might cause portability problems. See \ldblquote Mixing Classes Compiled on\par
Different Versions of the Java Platform\rdblquote  for more information.\par
Explanations for these warnings should describe why all application classes\par
are not compiled against the same Java platform version (e.g., your\par
application might require the use of 3rd party libraries which were compiled\par
against a previous JDK version).\par
\i\fs28 Warning: Unsupported Pluggable Look and Feel\par
\i0\fs20 The Swing classes in the Java 2 SDK and the JFC standard extension for JDK\par
1.1 software define a pluggable look and feel architecture that may cause\par
portability problems when an unsupported or unavailable LookAndFeel is\par
hard-coded into your application.\par
Explanations for this warning should show that the proper allowances for\par
unavailable and/or unsupported LookAndFeel have been made in your\par
application.\par
\f2 4 - 4 100% Pure Java Cookbook\par
\cf2\b\fs24 Explanations of Purity Problems and Variances\par
\cf1\b0\i\f0\fs32 Errors\par
\fs28 Error: Constant Strings with OS-Specific Syntax Used in I/O Class\par
Constructors\par
\i0\fs20 Use of strings including OS-specific constants, such as hardcoded line\par
termination characters or device names, are not portable.\par
\i\fs28 Error: Peer Use\par
\i0\fs20 The AWT peer classes are not intended for direct use by Java applications.\par
\i\fs28 Error: Use of Native Code\par
\i0\fs20\bullet  Native method definition\par
\bullet  Use of \f3 java.lang.Runtime.load\par
\f0\bullet  Use of \f3 java.lang.Runtime.loadLibrary\par
\f0 100% Pure Java programs cannot depend on native code.\par
\i\fs28 Error: Use of \i0\f3\fs29 java.lang.Runtime.exec\i\f0\fs28 .\par
\i0\fs20 Execution of an external application is not platform independent, except\par
under the circumstances detailed in the sections of this chapter that deal with\par
Variance Numbers 1, 5, and 17.\par
\i\fs28 Error: Injection into Core Library\par
\i0\fs20 100% Pure Java applications may not insert classes into the Java core library\par
(e.g. packages whose names start with \ldblquote\f3 java\f0\rdblquote , \ldblquote\f3 javax\f0\rdblquote , or \ldblquote\f3 omg\f0\rdblquote ).\par
\i\fs28 Error: Undefined Reference\par
\i0\fs20 An incomplete application (i.e., an application with dependencies on missing\par
or unavailable class files) cannot, obviously, be completely checked. It will be\par
reported as an error, because that is the most conservative estimate of what\par
might be in the missing class files. To remove these errors, simply include the\par
required classes to your list of \f3 .class \f0 files that JavaPureCheck parses.\par
\i\fs28 Error: Undocumented Internal \i0\f3\fs29 sun.*\i\f0\fs28 Class\par
\i0\fs20 Use of \f3 sun.* \f0 classes is not allowed by 100% Pure Java applications because\par
these classes are not guaranteed to be available on all platforms.\par
\f2 Revision Date: 10/00 4 - 5\par
\cf2\b\fs24 Variances\par
\cf1\b0\i\f0\fs40 Variances\par
\i0\fs20 Errors generated by the JavaPureCheck tool should include a Variance number\par
as part of the explanation in the JavaPureCheck tool report. Applications\par
containing errors without explanations are not compliant with the purity\par
standard.\par
Variances were previously granted to developers making variance requests to\par
Sun\rquote s Variance Council.\par
\i\fs32 Types of Variances\par
\i0\fs20 Variances are classified into three types:\par
\bullet  \b General Variances: \b0 General variances are available to all developers, and are\par
accepted as valid explanations for JavaPureCheck errors, and still being\par
compliant to the purity standard.\par
\bullet  \b Restricted Variances: \b0 Restricted variances are available to all developers.\par
Restricted variances typically have the potential for abuse that might\par
compromise application portability.\par
\i\fs32 The Variances\par
\i0\fs20 The following variances have been collected into groups. All variances listed\par
are general variances unless marked \ldblquote RESTRICTED.\rdblquote  The few specific\par
variances that have been granted are not identified due to their limited\par
applicability.\par
\b\i Note: \i0 Variances are numbered according to the order they were received as\par
variance requests from developers. Numbers that do not appear in the lists\par
below are associated with variance requests that were either denied,\par
dropped, or granted as Specific Variances (see above).\par
\fs24 V\fs19 ARIANCES FOR \fs24 P\fs19 ROGRAM \fs24 I\fs19 NVOCATION\par
\b0\fs20 Invoking another program by using either \f3 Runtime.exec() \f0 or\par
\f3 Method.invoke() \f0 is normally not portable, especially when the invoked\par
program itself is not pure. However, in most situations, portability will not be\par
compromised if the following criteria are followed:\par
\bullet  The invocation must be a direct result of a specific user action; the user must\par
know they\rquote re executing a separate application.\par
\bullet  The user must be able to choose, by configuration or as part of the invocation\par
action, which program gets invoked.\par
\f2 4 - 6 100% Pure Java Cookbook\par
\cf2\b\fs24 Explanations of Purity Problems and Variances\par
\cf1\b0\f0\fs20\bullet  A failure of the \f3 Runtime.exec() \f0 or \f3 Method.invoke() \f0 methods, especially\par
one caused by the absence of the requested program, must be handled cleanly.\par
\bullet  Use of this invocation is platform neutral. No platform specific functionality\par
documented by your product may be made available only to users of any\par
particular hardware/OS platform.\par
See \ldblquote Using Runtime.exec\rdblquote  in Chapter 2 for more information about these\par
variances.\par
\i\fs28 Variance: #1\emdash Invoking a Compiler\par
\i0\fs20 A compiler may be invoked using \f3 Runtime.exec() \f0 as long as the above\par
mentioned criteria are met. This variance also applies to the use of\par
\f3 Method.invoke() \f0 when used in a manner similar to \f3 Runtime.exec()\f0 .\par
\b Application of Variance: \b0 Variance #1 may be used as an explanation for errors\par
or warnings generated when these methods are used to start a compiler. A\par
statement must be included in the explanation that says:\par
\bullet  Invocation of the compiler is triggered by a user action.\par
\bullet  The specific compiler invoked can be selected (or preselected at installation\par
time) by the user.\par
\bullet  Any failure due to absence of the compiler is handled cleanly.\par
\bullet  No documented platform-specific functionality is made available.\par
\b\i Note: \i0 This error may be reported as \ldblquote Requires explanation; see Program\par
Invocation Variance.\rdblquote  Use Variance #1 for compiler invocation errors and\par
warnings.\par
\b0\i\fs28 Variance: #5\emdash Invoking a Browser\par
\i0\fs20 A browser may be invoked using \f3 Runtime.exec() \f0 as long as the above\par
mentioned criteria are met. This variance also applies to the use of\par
\f3 Method.invoke() \f0 when used in a manner similar to \f3 Runtime.exec()\f0 .\par
\b Application of Variance: \b0 Variance #5 may be used as an explanation for errors\par
or warnings generated when these methods are used to start a Web browser\par
(e.g., to display help text). A statement must be included in the explanation\par
that says:\par
\bullet  Invocation of the compiler is triggered by a user action.\par
\bullet  The specific compiler invoked can be selected (or preselected at installation\par
time) by the user.\par
\bullet  Any failure due to absence of the compiler is handled cleanly.\par
\bullet  No documented platform-specific functionality is made available.\par
\f2 Revision Date: 10/00 4 - 7\par
\cf2\b\fs24 Variances\par
\cf1\i\f0\fs20 Note: \i0 This error may be reported as \ldblquote Requires explanation; see Program\par
Invocation Variance.\rdblquote  Use Variance #5 for browser invocation.\par
\b0\i\fs28 Variance: #17\emdash Invoking a Configurable User Action\par
\i0\fs20 A program that is specified by a user (e.g., a user-configurable menu\par
application) can be invoked using \f3 Runtime.exec() \f0 as long as the above\par
mentioned criteria are met. This variance also applies to the use of\par
\f3 Method.invoke() \f0 when used in a manner similar to \f3 Runtime.exec().\par
\b\f0 Application of Variance: \b0 Variance #17 may be used as an explanation for\par
errors or warnings generated when these methods are used to start a program\par
selected by a user (e.g., a program chosen from a user configurable menu). A\par
statement must be included in the explanation that says:\par
\bullet  Invocation of the program is triggered by a user action.\par
\bullet  The specific program invoked can be selected (or preselected at installation\par
time) by the user.\par
\bullet  Any failure due to absence of the program is handled cleanly.\par
\bullet  No documented platform specific functionality is made available.\par
\b\i Note: \i0 This error may be reported as \ldblquote Requires explanation; see Program\par
Invocation Variance.\rdblquote  Variance #5 is for browser invocation; use Variance #17\par
for application invocation errors and warnings.\par
\fs24 V\fs19 ARIANCES FOR \fs24 P\fs19 ROGRAMS \fs24 C\fs19 ONTAINING \fs24 N\fs19 ATIVE \fs24 C\fs19 ODE\par
\b0\fs20 The following variances address allowed uses of native code in pure programs.\par
\i\fs28 Variance: #45\emdash Use of Native Code to Enable Common Platform\par
Functionality ***RESTRICTED***\par
\i0\fs20 It is often desirable to have an application \ldblquote well integrated\rdblquote  into the host\par
operating system. For example, a server running on Windows NT could benefit\par
greatly by running as an NT service (i.e., so it responds to task-shutdown\par
events). Similarly, a server running on UNIX could benefit from receiving\par
signals like \f3 SIGHUP \f0 or \f3 SIGINT\f0 . Unfortunately, this cannot currently be done\par
on the Java platform without using native methods. At some time in the future,\par
Sun may provide a Java technology API for this, but in the meantime, a\par
workaround is needed.\par
This variance extends the purity criteria to allow the use of native code for\par
situations where Java technology API functionality cannot be accommodated\par
due to a limitation (e.g., security measures) in a particular OS. Native code can\par
be used to enable features on a specific Java platform when these features\par
\f2 4 - 8 100% Pure Java Cookbook\par
\cf2\b\fs24 Explanations of Purity Problems and Variances\par
\cf1\b0\f0\fs20 cannot be exposed using Java APIs, and those features are exposed on other\par
Java platforms without the use of native code. Proper application of this\par
variance can actually improve portability.\par
\b\i Note: \i0 It is never acceptable to add new platform-specific features in this\par
manner or to use native code for the purpose of improving performance.\par
\b0 For example, to connect to port 80 on UNIX, a process must have root\par
privileges; but since it is generally unacceptable for an program to run with\par
root privileges, the privileges must be reduced after the connection is made.\par
This can be accomplished via a native call to UNIX. On Mac and other\par
operating systems, connections to port 80 do not have this restriction. Thus, by\par
allowing a check for various UNIX platforms, a 100% Pure Java program can\par
call the appropriate native routines to ensure it can run on these otherwise\par
blocked platforms.\par
\b Application of Variance: \b0 Variance #45 is a RESTRICTED variance.\par
\i\fs28 Variance: #47\emdash Use of Class Libraries Containing Unused Native\par
Code or Unused External References ***RESTRICTED***\par
\i0\fs20 If your program has dependencies on a class library that contains native or\par
impure code \emdash  but your program never exercises this code \emdash  purity is not\par
compromised, and you can use this variance.\par
The rule of thumb for these cases is that you must be able to adequately prove\par
that a potential portability problem cannot occur.\par
\b Application of Variance: \b0 Variance #47 is a RESTRICTED variance.\par
\i\fs28 Variance: #67\emdash  Use of Netscape Navigator and Internet Explorer\par
Security Managers\par
\i0\fs20 For an applet to work outside the sandbox (i.e., access security-constrained\par
methods), it must be granted permission by the Java platform security\par
manager. The two most widely used browsers in the industry today are\par
Netscape Navigator and Internet Explorer. However, both of these browsers\par
implement security manager functionality in a proprietary manner that\par
requires calls to impure classes within the browser itself.\par
While these proprietary implementations are within the scope of the Java\par
platform specifications, true platform independence might be compromised by\par
any applet that makes use of these classes. In particular, API differences\par
existing between platform implementations and/or versions of these products\par
\emdash  although these are unlikely \emdash  are outside of Sun\rquote s control, so portability is\par
not assured to the same level of certainty as with Java technology APIs.\par
\f2 Revision Date: 10/00 4 - 9\par
\cf2\b\fs24 Variances\par
\cf1\b0\f0\fs20 Recognizing the need for this applet functionality and its low risk to\par
portability, Sun has granted Variance #67 to allow applets to directly call the\par
necessary APIs in Netscape Navigator\fs28\'99\fs20 and Internet Explorer to access\par
security constrained methods.\par
\b\i Note: \i0 It is not acceptable to add platform-specific functionality through this\par
variance. The result of all method calls should preserve platform-neutral\par
behavior.\par
\b0 Calls to these proprietary APIs should produce \ldblquote Undefined Reference\rdblquote  errors\par
when tested by the JavaPureCheck tool. Use of stub or dummy classes to mask\par
calls to these APIs is not permitted.\par
Use of this variance is acceptable as long as care is taken to minimize the risk\par
of the Java virtual machine accidentally trying to load a class that does not\par
exist (which would cause a runtime error). This is best done by isolating calls\par
for each of these browsers into separate classes, and only loading those classes\par
that are applicable to the active browser.\par
\b Application of Variance: \b0 Variance #67 can be used as an explanation for\par
\ldblquote Undefined Reference\rdblquote  errors or warnings generated when making calls to\par
security methods in Netscape Navigator and Internet Explorer. Include in your\par
explanation a statement specifying that no platform specific functionality is\par
enabled by these calls other than to gain the necessary security permissions\par
required. Contact Netscape or Microsoft for information about developing to\par
their APIs.\par
\b\i Note: \i0 Compliance is always measured using Java Compatible platforms.\par
Specific code written to enable a browser\rquote s ability to access secure methods\par
will only be exercised if that browser uses a Java Compatible runtime\par
environment. See the list of recommended platforms in Appendix A,\par
"Available Java Platforms" for more information.\par
\b0\i\fs32 Miscellaneous Variances\par
\i0\fs20 The following variances address special cases that do not fall into a particular\par
category.\par
\i\fs28 Variance: #44\emdash  Use of Transitional Beans in 1.0.2 Applications\par
\i0\fs20 Transitional beans are a set of APIs used with JDK 1.0.2 to implement bean-like\par
functionality on the 1.0.2 Java platform. Sun discourages the use of these\par
classes since the JavaBeans architecture available on JDK 1.1 software (and\par
later Java platform versions) is much more robust.\par
\f2 4 - 10 100% Pure Java Cookbook\par
\cf2\b\fs24 Explanations of Purity Problems and Variances\par
\cf1\b0\f0\fs20 A transitional bean must implement the following classes:\par
\f3\bullet  sunw.io.Serializable\par
\bullet  sunw.util.EventObject\par
\bullet  sunw.util.EventListener\par
\f0 See \f3 http://java.sun.com/beans/initial.html \f0 for more information\par
about transitional beans.\par
\b Application of Variance: \b0 Variance #44 can be used as an explanation for errors\par
or warnings generated when adding the \f3 sunw.* \f0 classes necessary to\par
implement transitional beans. In addition, you must provide an explanation\par
stating that the classes are used for transitional beans purposes, and explain\par
why the code cannot be implemented as a \ldblquote real\rdblquote  bean.\par
\i\fs28 Variance: #68\emdash  Use of Java Platform-Specific (Hybrid) Functionality\par
\i0\fs20 Programs can be designed that run differently, depending upon the version of\par
the Java platform active during runtime. These programs are called hybrid\par
applications and can be classified as a 100% Pure Java program on each\par
targeted Java platform version.\par
For example, a hybrid program could use AWT calls when the program runs\par
on Java platform version 1.1, but use Java 2D\fs28\'99\fs20 calls when it runs on a 1.2\par
platform. The JavaPureCheck tool reports errors for the Java 2D API alls when\par
it tests the program using the 1.1 system model because the Java 2D APIs are\par
not defined for the 1.1 platform.\par
The JavaPureCheck tool will generate \ldblquote Undefined Reference\rdblquote  errors for any\par
API call made that does not exist in the selected system model.\par
Use of this variance is acceptable as long as care is taken to minimize the risk\par
of the Java virtual machine accidentally trying to load a class that does not\par
exist (which would cause a runtime error). This is best done by isolating\par
differences in application logic into separate classes and only loading those\par
classes which are applicable to a particular Java platform version.\par
\b\i Note: \i0 Making direct calls to a method and catching\par
\f4 ClassNotFoundException \f0 errors is not sufficient for this purpose.\par
Application of Variance: \b0 Variance #68 can be used as an explanation for\par
undefined reference errors on hybrid programs only. A description of how the\par
undefined reference has been isolated from affected Java platform versions\par
must be included in the explanation.\par
\f2 Revision Date: 10/00 A - 1\par
\i\f0\fs60 Available Java Platforms\par
A\par
\i0\fs20 You should execute your program on multiple Java platforms as a Dynamic\par
Test of your program for purity. Your program should run to completion and\par
demonstrate common behavior of all major features on at least three unique\par
Java platforms. A Java platform is a combination of the operating system and a\par
Java Compatible implementation of the Java application environment.\par
\i\fs40 Platforms for Java Applications and Applets\par
\i0\fs20 Tables A-1, A-3, and A-5 list the available platforms for the 1.0, 1.1, and 1.2\par
versions of the Java application environment used to verify the purity of Java\par
applications, servlets, beans and class libraries.\par
Tables A-2, A-4, and A-6 list the available platforms and browsers for the 1.0,\par
1.1, and 1.2 versions of the Java application environment used to verify the\par
purity of Java applets.\par
\b\i Note: \i0 Servlets should be verified using the Servlet Development Kit which\par
is available from \f4\fs18 http://java.sun.com/products/servlet/index.html.\par
\b0\f2\fs20 A - 2 100% Pure Java Cookbook\par
