\fs40 Frequently Asked Questions\par
\b\i0\fs20 Question 1: How do I explain all the bogus \ldblquote possible hard-coded path name\rdblquote\par
warnings I get with the JavaPureCheck tool?\par
\b0 Write a brief statement explaining that it is not a hard-coded path (if obvious)\par
or a description of the string usage. The intent of your explanation is to show\par
that the warning does not identify a potential portability problem.\par
\b Examples:\par
\b0\f3 Class :MyClass1\par
\fs18 Warning: possible hard-coded path: big/small\par
Note :Defines a bad path\par
Explanation :This is not a pathname.\par
\fs20 Class :MyClass2\par
\fs18 Warning: possible hard-coded path: product/big/pricing\par
Note :Defines a bad path\par
Explanation :This is part of a URL string.\par
\b\f0\fs20 Question 2: How do I explain the Class.forName warnings?\par
\b0 List each class that could be referenced by this statement. If your code allows a\par
user to select or input the class being loaded (e.g., the class is user\par
configurable), it is sufficient to explain how the class is chosen.\par
\b Examples:\par
\b0\f3 Class :com.myorg.foo.bar\par
\fs18 Warning: method reference:\par
\f2\fs20 B - 2 100% Pure Java Cookbook\par
\cf2\b\fs24 Frequently Asked Questions for Purity\par
\cf1\b0\f3\fs18 java.lang.Class.forName(java.lang.String)\par
Note :May load impure class\par
Explanation :The following classes can be loaded by this\par
statement and all are included as part of my\par
verification package:\par
com.myorg.Class1\par
com.myorg.Class2a\par
com.myorg.Class2b\par
com.myorg.Class3\par
\fs20 Class :com.abc.x123\par
\fs18 Warning: method reference:\par
java.lang.Class.forName(java.lang.String)\par
Note :May load impure class\par
Explanation :This class is selected by the user from a\par
menu that is preconfigured at install time.\par
\b\f0\fs20 Question 3: I get Class.forName warnings in places I don't use it. What's up?\par
\b0 The JavaPureCheck tool is not always able to distinguish how calls to this\par
method were initiated. For example, the fragment of source code\par
\f3 return java.lang.System.class;\par
\f0 results in the execution of the following bytecodes (or equivalent)\par
\f3 ldc #1 <String "java.lang.System">\par
invokestatic #12 <Method\par
java.lang.ClassforName(java.lang.String)>\par
areturn\par
\f0 Currently, the JavaPureCheck tool is not sophisticated enough to tell this code\par
from the very similar\par
\f3 return Class.forName(cn);\par
\f0 which results in\par
\f3 aload_1\par
invokestatic #12 <Method\par
java.lang.Class.forName(java.lang.String)>\par
areturn\par
\f0 Another example is the following code, which will also generate the warning:\par
\f3 public class test\par
\{\par
\f2 Revision Date: 10/00 B - 3\par
\cf2\b\fs24 Frequently Asked Questions\par
\cf1\b0\f3\fs18 Class c = test.class;\par
\fs20\}\par
\f0 Enter an explanation for the warning; the relevant line of source code would be\par
a perfectly acceptable explanation. It's important that the explanation tell the\par
class to which the \f3\fs18 .class \f0\fs20 operator is being applied. If the class is not part of\par
the core set of Java technology APIs, then it is probable that the \f3 forName\par
\f0 method is being used in a way which will require a much more detailed\par
explanation.\par
\b Example:\par
\b0\f3 Class :com.myorg.foo.bar\par
\fs18 Warning: method reference:\par
java.lang.Class.forName(java.lang.String)\par
Note :May load impure class\par
Explanation :This is not a use of method Class.forName.\par
The warning is being generated for the source statement:\par
return java.lang.System.class\par
\b\f0\fs20 Question 4: How do I mark warnings and errors that exist in pure 3rd party\par
products I use in my code?\par
\b0 If the product is certified, mark each warning and error as being dependent on\par
a pure 3rd party product.\par
\b Example:\par
\b0\f3 Class :sunsoft.jws.visual.rt.type.Converter\par
\fs18 Warning: method reference:\par
java.lang.Class.forName(java.lang.String)\par
Note :May load impure class\par
Explanation :This warning occurs in Sun's Java WorkShop\par
(Version 2.0) libraries. According to information on\par
http://java.sun.com/100percent, this library is\par
certified as 100% Pure Java.\par
\b\f0\fs20 Question 5: How do I mark warnings and errors that exist in non-certified 3rd\par
party products I use in my code?\par
\b0 If the product has not been certified, you might have to contact the vendor for\par
explanations. Please encourage these vendors to make their products\par
compliant with Sun\rquote s purity standard. Remember that any and all classes that\par
you use, rely on, or call, must pass the JavaPureCheck tool testing and have\par
appropriate purity explanations in order to be considered compliant with Sun\rquote s\par
purity standard.\par
\f2 B - 4 100% Pure Java Cookbook\par
\cf2\b\fs24 Frequently Asked Questions for Purity\par
\cf1\f0\fs20 Question 6: I can't get the JavaPureCheck tool to work. Help!\par
\b0 The JavaPureCheck tool will run on any 1.1 or later version Java Compatible\par
runtime.\par
Prior to running the JavaPureCheck tool, you can add the\par
\f3 javapurecheck.jar \f0 file to your \f3\fs18 CLASSPATH \f0\fs20 statement. The syntax for\par
executing the JavaPureCheck tool once your \f3\fs18 CLASSPATH \f0\fs20 is set is:\par
\f3 java JavaPureCheck\par
\f0 Alternatively, you can also set the classpath on your java commandline.\par
Consult your Java runtime documentation for details..\par
\b Question 7: Why does the JavaPureCheck tool take so long to process my files,\par
no matter how much memory I give it? What can I do?\par
\b0 The JavaPureCheck tool does code checking that requires method simulation.\par
This can be particularly memory intensive, and can also take time. Generally,\par
the speed of the JavaPureCheck tool depends entirely on the number of class\par
files that you give it, the class sizes(physical file size), and the number of static\par
strings defined in each class file.\par
The GUI interface of the JavaPureCheck tool can be rather memory intensive as\par
well. This is especially true when clicking on the \ldblquote details\rdblquote  button to view\par
purity warnings or errors because of the additional information that is being\par
displayed.\par
If you find that the JavaPureCheck tool seems to \ldblquote hang\rdblquote  at this point, run its\par
command line version instead. The documentation that comes with the\par
JavaPureCheck tool explains how to do this. You will still need to use the \f3 -ms\par
\f0 and \f3 -mx \f0 Java virtual machine switches, but this should enable you to check\par
your product and any third party classes that it uses more quickly.\par
When the JavaPureCheck tool has finished running, it will output a file called\par
\f3\fs18 RESULTS.JPC\f0\fs20 . This file can be viewed and edited by any standard text editor.\par
Optionally, you can still run the GUI, but click on \ldblquote exit\rdblquote  rather than viewing\par
the \ldblquote details\rdblquote  from within the GUI. The \f3 RESULTS.JPC \f0 file will be available on\par
disk and you can view or edit it in a text editor.\par
\b Question 8: When running the JavaPureCheck tool, I see a number of\par
\ldblquote undefined reference\rdblquote  errors that point to classes that are not mine.\par
\b0 Example:\par
\f3 Class :com.myclasses.classXYZ\par
\fs18 Error: undefined reference:\par
com.thirdparty.class123.Init()\par
Note :Undefined reference.\par
Explanation : <Explanation required>\par
\f2\fs20 Revision Date: 10/00 B - 5\par
\cf2\b\fs24 Frequently Asked Questions\par
\cf1\b0\f3\fs18 Status :ERROR\par
\f0\fs20 Think of the \ldblquote undefined reference\rdblquote  error as a message stating: \ldblquote incomplete\par
product\rdblquote  or \ldblquote classes missing\rdblquote . It is important to remember when running the\par
JavaPureCheck tool that you must include all of the classes that your product\par
calls, uses, or relies upon. An \ldblquote undefined reference\rdblquote  indicates that the\par
JavaPureCheck tool cannot find one or more of these classes. Remember that\par
the \f3 CLASSPATH \f0 will not be used to resolve these undefined references, and\par
that they must be included in your JavaPureCheck test. Just add the missing\par
class(es) to the JavaPureCheck list of classes to check and the error should go\par
away.\par
\b Question 9: When I run the JavaPureCheck tool on my classes, I get an\par
\ldblquote undefined reference\rdblquote  error pointing to the same class.\par
Example:\par
\b0\f3 Class :com.myclasses.classFoobar\par
\fs18 Error: undefined reference: com.myclasses.classFoobar\par
Note :Undefined reference.\par
Explanation : <Explanation required>\par
Status :ERROR\par
\f0\fs20 This error sometimes occurs with \f3 .class \f0 files that have been \ldblquote modified\rdblquote  after\par
being compiled. Specifically, the problem might occur after running a \ldblquote post\par
processor\rdblquote  (e.g.. an obfuscator or optimizer) on your complied files. If you run\par
into this problem, please contact the post-processorvendor for assistance.\par
\b Question 10: I get an error for the use of \ldblquote Runtime.exec()\rdblquote  in my class files. Is\par
this permitted?\par
\b0 This issue is completely documented in \ldblquote Using Runtime.exec\rdblquote  in Chapter 2. To\par
summarize, use of \f3 Runtime.exec() \f0 is permitted only when certain\par
conditions are met. The exact criteria for use of the \f3 Runtime.exec() \f0 method\par
are:\par
\bullet  The invocation must be a direct result of a specific user action; the user must\par
know they're executing a separate application.\par
\bullet  The user must be able to choose, by configuration or as part of the invocation\par
action, which application is executed.\par
\bullet  A failure of the \f3 Runtime.exec() \f0 method, especially one caused by the\par
absence of the requested application, must be handled cleanly.\par
For each class that uses \f3 Runtime.exec() \f0 you must include information\par
concerning each of these aspects to demonstrate that you meet the defined\par
criteria.\par
\f2 B - 6 100% Pure Java Cookbook\par
\cf2\b\fs24 Frequently Asked Questions for Purity\par
\cf1\f0\fs20 Question 11: Can a developer call all future releases of a certified program\par
100% Pure Java without undergoing further tests?\par
\b0 No. If you release a new version of the product that is for bug fixes only, you\par
should run JavaPureCheck on the updated product. If the new version includes\par
bug fixes and new features, functionality or enhancements, then the product\par
must be verified again to be considered compliant with the purity standard.\par
\b Question 12: What Variances requests are allowble?\par
\b0\bullet  (#1) Use of \f3 Runtime.exec \f0 or \f3 Method.invoke \f0 to start a compiler.\par
\bullet  (#5) Use of \f3 Runtime.exec \f0 or \f3 Method.invoke \f0 to start a browser.\par
\bullet  (#17) Use of \f3 Runtime.exec \f0 or \f3 Method.invoke \f0 to perform a configurable\par
user action.\par
\bullet  (#44) Use of transitional beans in 1.0.2 applications.\par
\bullet  (#45) Use of native code to enable common functionality (e.g., allowing access\par
to port 80 on UNIX) -- RESTRICTED.\par
\bullet  (#47) Use of class libraries containing unused native code or unused external\par
references --RESTRICTED.\par
\bullet  (#67) Use of Netscape\f1\'d4 \f0 and Internet Explorer security managers.\par
\bullet  (#68) Inclusion of Java platform version-specific (hybrid) functionality.\par
\b\i Note: \i0 These variances are fully explained in the \ldblquote Variances\rdblquote  section of this\par
guide.\par
Question 13: What Variance requests are not acceptable?\par
\b0\bullet  Use of native code with pure workarounds for unsupported platforms.\par
\bullet  Any use of \f3 sun.* \f0 packages.\par
\bullet  Use of optional native libraries as plug-in replacements for pure libraries.\par
\bullet  Use of external class dependencies (e.g., JavaScript support in browser).\par
\bullet  Dependencies on non-portable (i.e., impure) standard extensions.\par
\b Question 14: When running the JavaPureCheck tool, I note that some of my\par
classes are missing from the results but I know that I included them because\par
they are in the same directory as all of the other classes. I also will sometimes\par
see the message \ldblquote error: bad class file\rdblquote  appear in the console where the\par
JavaPureCheck tool is being run. What's happening?\par
\b0 This problem generally occurs because the file was compiled incorrectly or has\par
been modified in such a way as to be invalid. We have seen this issue occur\par
when compiling using Microsoft Visual J++ and marking the \ldblquote debug\rdblquote  option.\par
If you remove this option, then re-compile your classes, the \ldblquote bad class file\rdblquote\par
message should go away.\par
\b Question 15: Can I evaluate programs with dependencies on \ldblquote optional\par
packages\rdblquote ?\par
\b0 Yes and no. Java optional packages are implementations of Sun authorized\par
APIs that extend the core Java platform. These implementations can, in some\par
cases, be portable and be written completely in the Java application\par
\f2 Revision Date: 10/00 B - 7\par
\cf2\b\fs24 Frequently Asked Questions\par
\cf1\b0\f0\fs20 programming language, or they may be non-portable and make specific\par
platform dependent references to native code or to the underlying operating\par
system. Only programs bundled with portable optional packages can be\par
considered compliant with the purity standard.\par
Portable optional packages are normally considered compliant with the 100%\par
Pure Java standard. When bundled with a dependent program seeking\par
certification, these portable optional packages are treated in the same manner\par
as pure class libraries or beans.\par
\b\i Note: \i0 Sun has defined an exception to this \ldblquote must bundle\rdblquote  requirement for\par
the Servlet optional package. Because the Servlet API is normally\par
implemented as part of a web server, it is not reasonable to bundle a 100%\par
Pure Java program implementation of the API with every servlet because\par
there is no guaranteed way to invoke it.\par
Question 16: Can I use native JDBC drivers with my product?\par
\b0 Yes, native JDBC drivers can be included in your program as long as you\par
certify your program using a pure driver (i.e., references to native drivers may\par
not be exclusively hard-coded into your program). See \ldblquote Pitfall: "JDBC\'99 Driver\par
Loading" \rdblquote  in Chapter 3 for more information about using JDBC drivers.\par
You can either include a 100% Pure Java certified driver in your verification\par
package for certification, or use an uncertified (but pure) driver that will be\par
included as part of the static check of your program.\par
\b Question 17: How can my applet work outside the sandbox (i.e., access\par
security-constrained methods) in Netscape Navigator and Internet Explorer?\par
\b0 Use Variance #67. This variance allows your applet to make direct calls to the\par
native Netscape and Microsoft security manger APIs. See \ldblquote Variance: #67\emdash Use\par
of Netscape Navigator and Internet Explorer Security Managers.\rdblquote  Contact\par
Netscape or Microsoft for more information about developing to these APIs.\par
\b\i Note: \i0 Verification of your program to the purity standard should always be\par
done using Java Compatible platforms. Specific code written to enable a\par
browser\rquote s ability to access secure methods will only be exercised if that\par
browser uses a Java Compatible runtime environment. See Appendix A,\par
"Available Java Platforms" for information about the platforms used for\par
certification.\b0\par
